  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nLate this morning, water vapor satellite imagery and upper air \nanalysis showed a southern stream upper trough across central CO and\nwestern NM. A northern stream upper trough was located across the \nnorthern high Plains. A broad upper level ridge was located off the \neastern coast of the US. A southern stream upper level trough was \nslowly moving east across FL.\n\nThe 17Z surface map showed a cold front extending from eastern MN, \nsouthwest across northwest IA, then across eastern NE, then southwest \nacross western KS into northeast NM.\n\nToday through Tonight:\n\nThe southern stream H5 trough across NM will lift east-northeast \nacross the southern Plains and will phase with the northern stream \nH5 trough shifting east across the northern Plains. Ascent ahead of \nthe H5 trough and surface convergence ahead of the cold front will \ncause numerous showers and scattered thunderstorms to develop during\nthe late afternoon hours across central, north central and northeast\nKS. MUCAPES will only be in the 100 to 250 J/KG range, so I do not \nexpect any severe thunderstorms. If a stronger updraft manages to \ntransfer stronger winds aloft downward through the downdraft, then \nthere may be some gusty winds of 30 to 50 MPH in some of the isolated\nstronger updrafts. However, lapse rates are not that steep, so I \ndon't expect much in the way momentum transport to the surface. PWs \nwill be around 1 inch across the central counties of the CWA, with \n0.5\" across the far southeast counties and far western counties of \nthe CWA.\n\npost frontal stratiform rain will continue into the evening hours \nbut should shift east of the area through the early morning hour of \nSunday. Winds behind the front will increase from the north-\nnorthwest at 15 to 25 MPH with gusts of 30 to 35 MPH.\n\nSunday through Thursday:\n\nExpect dry and colder weather. A southern stream H5 trough will dig \nsoutheast across TX, and will remain well south of the CWA on \nTuesday. A surface ridge of high pressure will build southeast across \neastern KS through mid week. Highs will only reach into the 40s and \novernight lows will drop into the lower to mid 20s. The cold \novernight lows should kill any leftover insects. Any outdoor plants \nwill need to be moved indoors.\n\nThursday night through Saturday:\n\nThe extended range models are in fair agreement digging an H5 trough \nfrom the northern Plains into the mid MS River Valley by Friday \nmorning, then lifting the upper low northeast into the eastern \nGreat Lakes Friday night. The GFS is more amplified, developing an \nupper low across central MO Thursday night. The ECMWF shows more of \nan open H5 wave. Both models keep the stronger ascent east of the \nCWA. Plus, there is very little low-level moisture across the \ncentral Plains, so expect dry conditions. \n\nThe ECMWF shows a secondary H5 trough digging southeast across NE \ninto MO Friday night. The ECMWF shows some light QPF across eastern \nKS Friday afternoon and early evening. Temperatures will be warm \nenough for scattered rainshowers. \n\nHigh temperatures to end the week will warm into the 50s. Overnight \nlows will be in the mid to upper 20s.",
